russia has a hold over the west because  of the energy it supplies to it  forty percent of europe's gas for  example comes from russia  but moscow's reliance on fossil fuels  for income also makes it vulnerable  especially to a coordinated  international boycott the united states  is targeting the main artery of russia's  economy  we're banning all imports of russian oil  and gas and energy that means russian  oil will no longer be acceptable at u.s  ports and the american people will  deal another powerful blow to putin's  war machine  the uk will do the same with oil by the  end of the year we won't see the  elimination  of russian oil imports during the course  of the year this phased approach strikes  a good balance between eliminating uh  reliance on russian oil  but also allowing people time  and people in the market to adapt to the  new situation  even the european union despite its  reliance on russian energy is taking  steps to pull away  we can do it  and we can do it fast  all we need is the courage and grit  to get us there if ever there was a time  to do it it's now  russia is biting back  it has already threatened to stop gas  supplies to the west by the nordstream  one pipeline and warns of the further  consequences of a boycott it will be  much more expensive for european  consumers  in this scenario they will be the main  victims  european politicians should then  honestly warn their citizens consumers  what awaits them  and that the prices at gas stations for  electricity and heating will skyrocket  all the uncertainty and disruption is  already impacting consumers around the  world and at home here in the uk fuel  prices have soared to record highs the  average cost of diesel close to hitting  165 pence per liter and unleaded petrol  160.  although the uk is much less dependent  on russian energy than its european  counterparts the government must now  take steps to insulate this country from  foreign powers who weaponize energy  supply  in the coming days boris johnson will  set out his energy supply strategy and  that is likely to include new investment  in renewables and nuclear power  but he's also under pressure partly from  within his own party to start digging  for more oil and gas in the uk's north  sea and that may well happen  all other agendas for the moment  sidelined as the international community  scrambles to punish russian aggression  and stop a growing global energy crisis  hannah thomas peter sky news
 

